---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { CONTACT_EMAIL, ISSUES_URL, SOURCE_URL } from "../data/site";

const contact = CONTACT_EMAIL
  ? `mailto:${CONTACT_EMAIL}?subject=${encodeURIComponent("Accessibility")}`
  : ISSUES_URL;
---

<Base
  title="Accessibility statement: AGENCY OS"
  description="How this website is built to work by eye gaze, a single switch, a keyboard, a screen reader or a mouse, how it's tested, and how to report a problem."
  current="accessibility"
>
  <article class="wrap prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Accessibility statement</p>
    <h1>This site should work for everyone AGENCY is for</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      If you use AGENCY, you should be able to use this website the same way: by eye gaze, a single switch, a
      keyboard, a screen reader, or a mouse.
    </p>

    <h2 id="target">Our target</h2>
    <p>
      WCAG 2.2 at level AA, plus the bar AGENCY sets for itself: every standalone control is at least 96
      pixels tall with at least 24 pixels between controls, nothing has a time limit, and nothing moves under
      your gaze.
    </p>

    <h2 id="what">What we did</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Large, well-spaced buttons and links, with a thick, high-contrast focus outline.</li>
      <li>
        A “Skip to content” link, a logical heading order, and landmarks for the header, navigation and
        footer.
      </li>
      <li>
        A large “Next” link at the end of each section, so you can move through the page in steps instead of
        scrolling.
      </li>
      <li>
        Body text in Atkinson Hyperlegible, a typeface designed by the Braille Institute for low-vision
        readers, at a large base size; headings use your computer's own system font. The site's own copy of
        the font is used; nothing is loaded from another company.
      </li>
      <li>Light and dark colours, following your Mac's setting or your choice, both checked for contrast.</li>
      <li>
        Motion is short, gentle and plays once; nothing loops. It's turned off completely if you've asked your
        computer to reduce motion.
      </li>
      <li>
        Everything works without JavaScript except finding the newest download, which falls back to a link.
      </li>
      <li>The page works at 400% zoom and on phone screens without scrolling sideways.</li>
      <li>
        Screenshots have text descriptions. The demo video, when it exists, will have captions and never play
        by itself.
      </li>
      <li>Plain language, one idea at a time.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="tested">How it's tested</h2>
    <p>Every change to the site runs these checks automatically before it's published:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>axe</strong> (an automated accessibility checker) on every page, in light and dark, on desktop
        and phone sizes, with no violations allowed.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Keyboard tests</strong>: every control can be reached with Tab, the focus is always visible,
        and the download and questions work with the keyboard alone.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Size tests</strong>: every standalone control is at least 96 pixels tall with 24 pixels of
        space.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Lighthouse</strong>: an accessibility score of 100 is required on every page.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A link checker</strong>, so no link leads nowhere.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      Automated tools find many problems but not all of them. The site hasn't yet been tested by people using
      eye gaze, switches or screen readers day to day. That testing matters most, and we'd welcome it.
    </p>

    <h2 id="known">Known limitations</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        Links inside paragraphs are ordinary text size, as WCAG allows; every one is repeated or reachable as
        a large control nearby.
      </li>
      <li>
        The download panel needs JavaScript to find the newest version. Without it, the panel shows a large
        link to the releases page on GitHub. GitHub's own pages are not under our control.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2 id="report">Tell us about a problem</h2>
    <p>
      If anything on this site is hard to use, please tell us, and say how you were browsing if you're happy
      to share. We treat accessibility problems as bugs.
    </p>
    <p>
      <a class="btn" href={contact}>
        {CONTACT_EMAIL ? "Email us" : "Report it on GitHub"}
      </a>
    </p>
    <p>
      This statement was last reviewed on 11 October 2026. The site's source code is{" "}
      <a href={`${SOURCE_URL}/tree/main/site`}>on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
